Human exploration has purposes that elected officials may value, and NASA's account totals include changes in the activities they fund. The #cite(<omb2026>, form: "prose", supplement: [pp.~67–69]) also points to particular cost concerns, including Mars Sample Return's escalation, in defending the administration's priorities. Congress should weigh those arguments against the scientific work a redirection would displace. Congress should likewise name the NSF objectives it intends to protect and explain how the appropriation will support them. That would connect a claim about the budget to a decision about the research.

= Following the appropriation into research
NSF's 2025 terminations make that requirement concrete. The agency changed its treatment of broader impacts, the expected benefits of research beyond new knowledge. Following a preliminary injunction, it reinstated 114 awards to 45 institutions in June @nsfpriorities2025. Previously approved support was interrupted and then restored in this episode. A favorable agency-wide appropriation would not, on its own, tell Congress whether such a project could continue. That is where the oversight function discussed in the course becomes necessary.

The research on funding delays supplies a reason to ask about timing as well as totals. #cite(<tham2023>, form: "prose") finds that interrupted NIH renewals disrupt laboratory spending and that additional grants help laboratories absorb the interruption. Its publication estimates are not statistically distinguishable from zero; its clearest result concerns the resources available to keep work going. The employment findings of #cite(<tham2026>, form: "prose") extend that concern to careers. For NSF oversight, these studies support asking which teams have little alternative funding and how long they must wait when support is interrupted. Answering those questions would connect a preserved appropriation to the people it is meant to support.

I would make that connection part of Congress's explanation of its NSF funding decisions. Appropriators should compare the chosen amount with the president's request, an inflation-adjusted historical baseline, and the relevant authorized goals, then identify the activities they expect the agency to sustain or reduce. NSF's spending plans and subsequent reporting should make departures from those expectations visible, including terminations and reinstatements of awards. Congress already requires spending plans in the FY2026 funding framework @congress2026[sec.~528] @appropriators2026[pp.~1–3]. Committees can use them to question agency officials about the work supported by the appropriation and require explanations when implementation departs from the stated plan.

This would also give scientific advocates a more useful basis for approaching lawmakers. The American Astronomical Society encouraged members to thank Congress for the FY2026 settlement @aas2026, an example of the constituent mobilization discussed in the course @coursecongress2026[sec.~6.6.4]. The next request should be more specific: which research commitments will this funding sustain, and which will it put aside? Public answers would let researchers challenge an agency's decision or a legislator's priorities without treating every budget reduction as equally damaging.

= Conclusion
Congress's FY2026 NSF appropriation was a real use of its power of the purse. It prevented most of a proposed reduction that would have transformed the agency's funding. Yet the ten-year record shows why that success cannot carry the whole argument for congressional protection: purchasing power declined, and Congress did not finance its expansion plan at the authorized scale. I would judge Congress by whether it makes those choices explicit and follows the resulting resources into the research it says it supports. Congress can choose a smaller scientific program. It should then defend that choice in terms of the work Americans will be able to pursue, rather than relying on the larger cut it rejected.

